fection of 293  cells which is step one here  in these diagrams and the aav and  lentivirus is  collected post-transfection and is  further processed  okay so on to the meat of the talk which  is to discuss the ways we can improve  yield of aav and lentivirus through  optimizing this critical step in  production transfection  uh so before we get to the q a we will  discuss  these parameters okay  so first the reagent transit virus gen  reagent was developed by mirus  with this workflow in mind we first  screened our vast library of molecules  and compounds to identify  regions that would result in the highest  yields of aav and lentivirus  we used a reporter gfp system where the  aav2 or lentivirus contained  a gfp transgene secondly  we also wanted to ensure that our  reagent would be compatible for both  suspension  and adherent hec293 cells in either  serum containing or serum free  conditions  and another important parameter was that  the protocol for use of our reagent  would be robust  and scalable because we know how  important  consistency is to any process in  addition to getting a really great  titer  and so a couple of years ago in about  2017  we introduced our transit virus gen  reagent which accomplished  all of that and specifically to address  the needs of  our pre-clinical and commercial  manufacturing customers we launched our  select  format of the reagent this is going to  be closely followed by the launch this  year  of a completely gmp manufactured version  what's important to note is that all of  these formats  ruo select and gmp are identical in  formulation  so any development work done with the  transit virus gen  ruo regent can be applied seamlessly to  the use of the select and gmp transit  virus gen  reagent and together with the launch of  the gmp reagent will be the launch of  our virus  gen aav transfection kit and virus gen  lentivirus transfection kit or lv  transfection kit  these kits include enhancer solutions  which  further boost virus production these  kits will also be available  in ruo select and gmp formats  later this year okay  so we benchmarked our reagents against  other commonly used transfection  reagents that are pei based  so what i'm showing you here are  functional titers  for aav on the left and lentivirus on  the right  in suspension 293 culture and as you can  see the addition  of the enhancer component of the virus  gen kit  greatly improves the yield of aav  compared to competing reagents  and the same case for lentivirus  so which reagent that you use obviously  has a very  significant impact on vector yields but  so can  optimizing the cell culture conditions  of your 293 culture  in this experiment here we examine cell  density at the time of transfection for  xp293 cells  used for the production of aav and  we looked at using either the reagent  alone  or in the presence of the enhancer  component  shown here in red so as you can see the  cell density at the time of transfection  clearly impacts the resultant titers  especially for  when the enhancer component of the  viruschen aav kit is used  and for this particular system we would  recommend a cell density at transfection  of about three to four million cells per  mill  optimizing cell density also applies for  lentivirus production  and in this experiment um lentivirus  titers were highest  when cells were at 4 million cells  per ml at the time of transfection  shown here is data for an optimization  experiment that we performed for  adherent cells  they were t17s grown in serum containing  dmm  what's shown on the x-axis is the  feeding density  the day before and the images below show  the percent confluency at time of  transfection  again the titers are shown for the minus  and plus  enhancer component and in each case the  enhancer boosts titers  generally we do not recommend that cells  are over confluent  or covering the entire plate at the time  of transfection  also we don't recommend that they're at  a 