nd as you can see  the two to one ratio is clearly better  for aav tighter than the three to one  regardless of the amount of dna that was  used  for this particular experimental system  and aav vector  2 to 1 at 1.5 or 2 micrograms  produced the highest titers so when  you're considering doing an  optimization experiment like this you  could test different ratios  like 1.5 to 1 for example with a dna  titration  and gmp manufactured dna it can be an  expensive resource  so optimizing dna amounts used is  definitely  also important for this reason  next another interesting parameter to  consider which  may not be on your radar is complex  formation conditions  and by complex formation i mean the  process of mixing  dna with transfection reagent the  resultant dna reagent complex is what is  added to cells  and so in this aav production experiment  we examined the temperature of complex  formation  as well as the time we allowed complexes  to form before we added them to cells  as you can see uh high aav titers can be  obtained  from complexes that were formed at  either four degrees  or room temperature at four degrees we  think complexes grow a bit slower  for this experimental system and vector  we'd recommend  an incubation time of about 30 minutes  and i also just want to make two notes  that are applicable to  all culture systems and transfection  with transit virus gen  and the first is that the transit virus  generation should not  sit in the complex formulation solution  by itself  for long periods of time so we recommend  generally that  the dna component is added first  followed by the reagent  if the reagent is added first the dna  should follow within five minutes  and the second point that i want to  make is that you do want to mix  well after each addition so after you  add dna mix well after you  add reagent mix well but afterwards we  don't recommend  additional mixing as additional  agitation can disrupt  the complex formation and form complexes  which may have a negative effect on  titers  and here is a similar complex formation  time course experiment we performed  for aav production using the virus gen  aav  kit with and without enhancer and  same idea here about 30 minutes was  optimal for yields for this experimental  system  playing around with other complex  formation conditions can  improve titers and importantly it should  be considered for  different culture and vector systems in  order to achieve  the highest titer  the last topic i wanted to touch on is  the last stage of transfection  not quite transfection itself but uh the  assessing  of viral vector titer and what i'm  showing you here  is a comparison of transfection for  lentivirus production  using the transit virus gen reagent  alone  or pei in adherent 293 t17  cells and this is data courtesy of dr  drobna  who produced lentivirus that had an rfp  reporter  one of the vectors contained a control  rfp  in the a panels and another vector  containing  a cas9 rfp fusion and the b  panels and these images show  rfp transgene expression most likely off  the transfer plasmid that was used  during transfection  and not from the lentivirus that was  being produced and secreted into the  supernatant  these images were captured 17 to 22  hours post transfection  looks great that the transfection of the  plasmids worked  and virusgen is kicking butt over pei  but transfection efficiency of the  plasmids used to produce virus it is  important  however i want to caution that using  transfection  efficiency is no substitute for titer  i think the data from this experiment  i'm going to share in the next few  slides really demonstrate that point  we transfected xp293 cells using the  virus gen aav kit with the enhancer  component  we also included a negative  untransfected condition  meaning no reagents or dna were added to  cells  and what we did was we transfected cells  with plasmas that are required for aav  production  so in one case we transfected a