u might want to consider optimizing  transfection on  a case-by-case basis by examining some  of the parameters that i pointed out  today and the protocols that are  provided by manufacturers are great  starting points  so at miras we are more than happy to  work with you to discuss  your unique workflows and systems and  requirements for transfection  so to summarize here are some potential  variables you could consider  for an optimization study obviously one  of the  important outputs in such a study would  be the viral titer with the goal being  to achieve maximal functional and  physical titer  a true study of a matrix of all these  variables  is a huge undertaking and here are some  of the variables  that i mentioned today  so going back to this image to remind  you why  you would even want to eke out more and  more virus  through transfection optimization  clearly quite a lot of virus  needs to be produced in order to support  gene and cell therapies today  and in the future so we can estimate  the amount of virus that is going to be  required per dose  so treatments whether they're  administered locally or systemic  will require a lot of virus and if we  take  yield as these ballpark estimates here  you can see that the virus required per  dose  gets to be quite high in the hundreds to  thousands of liter  range in the case of a systemic  treatment  and if you can achieve the same amount  of virus with  less cell culture and number of  transfections  this would be extremely helpful not only  for  material costs but also labor and  workflow costs  and especially precious time  so some concluding remarks here high  viral titers  are required for these exciting  therapies  and hopefully we're going to see more  and more of these therapies doing  amazing things in the years to come and  while transfection  is just one aspect of advancing these  therapies  i hope i've convinced you that  optimization of transfection is a  worthwhile endeavor to produce  the highest tider a virus in order to  support these gene and cell therapies  and lastly we are happy that our transit  virus and reagent alone  or together with the enhancer solutions  in the virus gen  aav and lv transfection kits can help in  this journey to  produce high tiders of aav and  lentiviral vectors  for these exciting therapies  if you are interested in testing the  transit virus gen reagent  and or the virus gen aav or lv kits  don't hesitate to reach out to either  myself directly or through tech support  by email or phone  as i mentioned earlier mirror scientists  are happy to  discuss your unique transfection  workflows  and with that i'll call it here and take  any questions  thank you so much sandy for that  informative presentation and we will now  start the live  q a portion of the webinar if you have  any questions you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box  located on the far left of your screen  so let's get started it looks like  sandy we already have some great  questions coming in from our audience  members  our first question why are the ratios  you start with  are or what what they are you use  one to one but then recommend one to 1.5  can you tell us a little more about that  uh yeah sure happy to explain that so  i'm not quite sure where you saw the one  to  one  but i think you're talking about the  microliter of reagent to dna ratio  and this is going to vary by what  experimental system you're using and  packaging next things like that  but essentially the the general range  that we came up with for our protocols  is based on the amount of reagent  that you can think of as a concentration  that is provided  and so um actually the ratio that we'd  recommend  working with first is closer to the  range of two to one  to three to one um and  anything that is dramatically different  from this like maybe  something crazy like 10 to 1 would  cause the complexes to form a little bit  too quickly something that's  dramatically smaller than this let's say  like .25 to one  would have the reverse problem so you  can definitely get away  with differing in the ratio there  but i would stick to the general  range of one to one to about maybe even  five to one  yes i hope that was your question if you  want to clarify more you can  feel free to add another question thank  you so much danny  now what is your complex formation  volume relative  to your total culture volume  yeah so i don't i didn't think i talked  about that but that is definitely  something  that can be optimized as well so to  repeat that's a complex formation volume  that's essentially the volume of the  solution that you use  that you use to form your complexes  where you add your dna  and the reagent to and in our protocols  it's generally around 10 percent  of the total culture volume so if your  culture volume  is 25 milliliters 10 of that would be  2.5  milliliters and we've done some  experiments where we  drop that down to five percent rather  than  10 percent and what we find is the  complex  size grows a bit quicker so if you do  want to play around  with your complex formation volume you  might also want to think about  how long you're allowing those complexes  to form  thank you so much and again i want to  thank our audience members for these  great questions  coming in our next question  our validation process requires that we  evaluate  multiple lots of reagent  is this something you would support  sandy  um yeah so that would depend on  so we have different formats of our  reagents they're the same formulation  but we have an ruo select and gmp  manufactured version and for the ruo  and select version we already have  several different lots available  so you can evaluate the different lots  of those the gmp  reagent which is going to be released  later this year about end of may  beginning of june  there is just one initial lot however  following the initial release i think in  a few months following we'll have an  additional two batches  so depending on which  format you want to evaluate yes there  will be multiple lots  to to test thank you so much we have  time for one more question  can you explain your process for  functionally  tightering virus yeah sure so  i kind of went over that in the  presentation already i mentioned that  for  the aav2 that we've been producing  the most in-house here at muras we've  been using ht 1080 cells  and a functional titering method  essentially is  trying to measure how much of the sample  can infect  a target cell so for the aav2  because ht1080s can be infected by them  we use hd1080s and what you do is you  can  count how many ht1080s are in a given  well you can apply  your virus directly to the well and what  you're trying to measure is  how much of those cells was infected  and those are called transduction units  and the the way that you can count  the number of cells that were infected  um what we do is a flow based or flow  cytometry based assay  um so if there's a gfp reporter gene in  uh packaged in your aav for instance or  lentivirus  you can count how many of those cells  are gfp positive using flow cytometry  and from there  you can relate that to  the number of viral particles that were  in your sample that are functional  and since you know the volume that you  applied to the well  you can get a concentration transduction  units per  milliliter of sample so you can  design your assay based on what's  packaged in your virus  so if you don't have a reporter gene  instead if you want to use flow  cytometry you can use an antibody that  finds your protein or gene that's being  expressed in your  in your virus thank you so much danny we  actually have time for one more  i saw another one come in how do you  plan to use this study in the world  like what do you hope to accomplish yeah  sure that's a  more um i guess a more abstract kind of  question but  i think it's easily answerable by the  kinds of  therapies t